\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Controlled Ablation Benchmark with Explicit Penalty Conditions: Prior Specificity, Weak-Regularization Mimicry, and Training-Proxy Lag Across Three Operational Wind Farms (Quantile Calibrated $r=2, q=1/3$).}}{12}{table.caption.14}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:controlled_ablation}{{2}{12}{Controlled Ablation Benchmark with Explicit Penalty Conditions: Prior Specificity, Weak-Regularization Mimicry, and Training-Proxy Lag Across Three Operational Wind Farms (Quantile Calibrated $r=2, q=1/3$)}{table.caption.14}{}}
\def\@LN@column{1}
\def\@LN@column{2}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Prospective Controlled Attribution Benchmark across Three Operational Wind Farms. (a) Prior-specific mapping effects under strong regularization on Penmanshiel ($N_{\text  {train}}=1{,}583$): authentic empirical prior guidance provides downside risk reduction ($-1.28\%$), whereas permuting the aerodynamic mapping (Pseudo-Scrambled prior) triggers a $+61.2\%$ surge in operational dispatch cost. (b) Weak-penalty numerical regularization mimicry on Kelmarsh ($N=5{,}098$), where genuine and scrambled priors yield closely matched outcomes at $\lambda = 0.01$, with no clear empirical separation and outcomes consistent with non-specific numerical regularization. (c) Monotonic cost inflation under training proxy staleness: dispatch cost escalation across all three farms as historical wind proxy lag $k$ in the training loss increases from 1\,h to 6\,h. (d) Decoupling of symmetric point error (RMSE) from asymmetric decision risk ($r=2$), demonstrating that point metrics mask tail operational hazards.}}{13}{figure.caption.16}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ablation_3d}{{2}{13}{Prospective Controlled Attribution Benchmark across Three Operational Wind Farms. (a) Prior-specific mapping effects under strong regularization on Penmanshiel ($N_{\text {train}}=1{,}583$): authentic empirical prior guidance provides downside risk reduction ($-1.28\%$), whereas permuting the aerodynamic mapping (Pseudo-Scrambled prior) triggers a $+61.2\%$ surge in operational dispatch cost. (b) Weak-penalty numerical regularization mimicry on Kelmarsh ($N=5{,}098$), where genuine and scrambled priors yield closely matched outcomes at $\lambda = 0.01$, with no clear empirical separation and outcomes consistent with non-specific numerical regularization. (c) Monotonic cost inflation under training proxy staleness: dispatch cost escalation across all three farms as historical wind proxy lag $k$ in the training loss increases from 1\,h to 6\,h. (d) Decoupling of symmetric point error (RMSE) from asymmetric decision risk ($r=2$), demonstrating that point metrics mask tail operational hazards}{figure.caption.16}{}}
